Likutay Halachos - Hilchos Shavuos 1

@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,600&family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700&display=swap');

:root { --primary:#2c1810;--accent:#8b4513;--gold:#b8860b;--bg:#faf6f0;--bg-alt:#f0ebe3;--border:#d4c5a9;--text:#2c1810;--text-light:#5a4a3a;--highlight:#fff8dc; }

*{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}

body{font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.85;color:var(--text);background-color:var(--bg);text-align:justify}

.container{max-width:780px;margin:0 auto;padding:40px 30px 60px}

.main-header{text-align:center;margin-bottom:35px;padding-bottom:25px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border)}

.main-header .book-title{font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:8px}

.main-header h1{font-size:32px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-bottom:6px}

.main-header .subtitle{font-size:20px;font-style:italic;color:var(--text-light);margin-bottom:4px}

.main-header .halacha-num{font-size:17px;color:var(--accent);font-weight:600}

.main-header .source-ref-header{font-size:15px;color:var(--text-light);font-style:italic;margin-top:6px}

.section-marker{text-align:center;margin:35px 0 20px;font-size:16px;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent);letter-spacing:2px}

.section-marker::before,.section-marker::after{content:"\2014";margin:0 12px;color:var(--border)}

p{margin-bottom:16px;text-indent:1.5em}

p.no-indent,.section-marker+p,.main-header+p{text-indent:0}

.verse{font-style:italic;color:var(--accent)}

.verse-source{font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);font-style:normal}

.heb-term{font-style:italic}

.term-def{font-size:15px;color:var(--text-light)}

.concept{font-weight:600}

.source-ref{font-size:15px;color:var(--text-light)}

.separator{text-align:center;margin:30px 0;color:var(--border);font-size:20px;letter-spacing:8px}

.gematria{background:var(--bg-alt);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:12px 18px;margin:20px auto;max-width:520px;text-align:center;font-size:16px;color:var(--accent)}

.gematria .heb{font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:700}

.summation{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f0e8 0%,#ebe5d8 100%);border:2px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;padding:25px 30px;margin:45px 0 20px}

.summation-header{text-align:center;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:15px;font-weight:700}

.summation p{font-size:16px;text-indent:0;margin-bottom:10px;color:var(--text-light)}

.flowchart{margin:30px auto;max-width:600px}

.flow-step{background:white;border:2px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;padding:12px 18px;text-align:center;font-size:15px;margin:0 auto;max-width:520px;color:var(--primary);font-weight:600}

.flow-arrow{text-align:center;font-size:22px;color:var(--gold);margin:6px 0}

.flow-result{background:var(--highlight);border:2px solid var(--gold);border-radius:8px;padding:12px 18px;text-align:center;font-size:15px;margin:0 auto;max-width:520px;color:var(--accent);font-weight:700}

.blessed{text-align:center;margin-top:30px;font-style:italic;color:var(--gold);font-size:17px;font-weight:600}

And therefore, the Torah is compared to wine and milk &mdash; which are the aspect of the purification of the souls, in the aspect of &#8220;blood becomes turbid,&#8221; etc. &mdash; from which Torah is made, as above. And this is &#8220;I have gathered my myrrh with my spice&#8221; &mdash; namely, the gathering and purification of the souls, as above. &#8220;I have drunk my wine with my milk&#8221; &mdash; namely, the Torah, which is compared to wine and milk, which are themselves the purification of the souls, in the aspect of &#8220;blood becomes turbid,&#8221; etc. And then Torah is made from them, as above.

And this is what is written: &#8220;Do not look upon wine when it reddens&#8221; &mdash; namely, the blemish of wine, in the aspect of blood, in the aspect of &#8220;when it reddens,&#8221; &#8220;for its end is blood&#8221; &mdash; this is the aspect of &#8220;do not look&#8221; (al taire) &mdash; the aspect of the blemish of Hashgachah. For when the wine is purified in the aspect of &#8220;if he merits, it makes him a head,&#8221; in the aspect of &#8220;blood becomes turbid and becomes milk&#8221; &mdash; which is the aspect of the wine and milk of the Torah, as above &mdash; then it is in the aspect of &#8220;a good eye&#8221; (tov ayin) &mdash; the aspect of the eyes of Hashgachah that are drawn through the aspect of wine and milk &mdash; namely, through the Torah. And this is the aspect of &#8220;His eyes&hellip; bathing in milk.&#8221;

And this is the aspect of &#8220;A good eye shall be blessed&#8221; (Mishlei 22:9) &mdash; the aspect of the cup of wine of blessing, which &#8220;requires that one place his eyes upon it&#8221; (B&#8217;rachos 51a) &mdash; the aspect of the eyes of Hashgachah that are drawn through good wine, wine of holiness, the aspect of &#8220;ruddy, with beautiful eyes&#8221; (Sh&#8217;muel I, 16:12), said regarding David, who is the aspect of the cup of blessing. But the blemish of wine, in the aspect of blood as above &mdash; is the opposite of this &mdash; the aspect of &#8220;do not look upon wine,&#8221; etc. &mdash; &#8220;do not look&#8221; specifically, as above.

And this is: &#8220;When he places his eye in the cup&#8221; (Mishlei 23:31) &mdash; &#8220;in the purse&#8221; (bakis) is [also how it could be] written &mdash; namely, the craving for wealth, which is the blemish of the bloods, the blemish of wine. For the essential purification is from within the craving for money, as above. For the craving for money is the aspect of dam niddah [the blood of impurity], the aspect of &#8220;And their gold as an impurity&#8221; (Yechezkel 7:19), as Rabainu, of blessed memory, wrote. And this is &#8220;when he places his eye in the cup&#8221; &mdash; &#8220;in the purse&#8221; is written &mdash; for they are one and the same aspect. For through the craving for money, which is the aspect of the blemish of the bloods, through this the wine is blemished, as above. And through this, the eyes are blemished &mdash; the aspect of the blemish of Hashgachah, as above. And this is the aspect of &#8220;He placed his eyes on his money&#8221; &mdash; that in exchange for the money, he gives his eyes. Namely, he blemishes the eyes &mdash; the aspect of the eyes of Hashgachah &mdash; through the craving for money, as above. For the Hashgachah is drawn through breaking the craving for money, as above.

And this is why we count seven weeks in order to merit Shavuos, to receive the Torah, through which Hashgachah is drawn &mdash; which is the aspect of the seven layers of the eye, which are the aspect of the seven days, the seven middos, as is known. And this is the aspect of the seven heads of the two Shinnim &mdash; the Shin of three heads and the Shin of four heads &mdash; which are the two intellects through which one receives the Torah (as explained there; see there). And corresponding to this, we count seven weeks, which are a preparation for receiving the Torah.

And therefore one must count days and count weeks &mdash; which are the aspect of seven lower days and seven upper days &mdash; namely, the seven lower days and the seven upper days, which are the aspect of the seven weeks, as brought in the Zohar. And they are the aspect of the two aforementioned intellects &mdash; which are the rectification of the upper Chariot and the lower Chariot &mdash; which are the aspect of seven days and seven days. For each intellect and each Chariot is comprised of the aspect of seven days and seven middos, as is known.

And because one needs to receive the Torah through the aforementioned two intellects &mdash; which are the upper intellect and the lower intellect &mdash; therefore we count days and weeks, which are the aspect of seven days and seven days &mdash; the aspect of the two intellects as above. And then one merits receiving the Torah on Shavuos, as above.

And this is: &#8220;Seven weeks you shall count for yourself; from the time the sickle begins [to cut] the standing crop, you shall begin to count&#8221; (D&#8217;varim 16:9), etc. For the S&#8217;firah is the gathering and purification of the sparks and the souls, as above. And this is &#8220;from the time the sickle begins [to cut] the standing crop&#8221; &mdash; from the time the harvest begins. For the omer permits the harvest &mdash; for the harvesting of grain from the fields is the aspect of the gathering and purification of the souls, as is known. And therefore they were forbidden to harvest before the omer, for it is forbidden to harvest them before their time. And their time is from the offering of the omer of barley specifically &mdash; for then the Sitra Achara is subdued, and then there is power to purify souls in the aspect of &#8220;I have gathered my myrrh,&#8221; etc. &mdash; which is the aspect of the omer, the aspect of S&#8217;firah, as above. And therefore, specifically then, one can harvest the grain from the fields &mdash; namely, to gather souls, as above.

And this is: &#8220;And you shall make a festival of weeks to Hashem, from the generosity of your hand that you shall give&#8221; (D&#8217;varim 16:10), etc. &mdash; namely, the aspect of tz&#8217;dakah, a generous spirit &mdash; through which the craving for money is broken, and through which one merits the festival of Shavuos &mdash; namely, receiving the Torah, as above.

And this is why we read [the book of] Rus on Shavuos &mdash; which is the revelation of the kingship of Mashiach, the kingship of David. For receiving the Torah, as above, is through the revelation of the kingship of Mashiach (as explained there in the teaching &#8220;Happy Is the People&#8221;) &mdash; that through breaking the craving for money, the aspect of Mashiach is revealed and one merits receiving the Torah. And therefore, on Shavuos, when we merit receiving the Torah through breaking the craving for money, as above, therefore, then is the aspect of the revelation of Mashiach, as above. And therefore we read Rus, as above.

And therefore, David passed away on Atzeres [= Shavuos] (Yerushalmi, Chagigah 2:3). For Atzeres is the aspect of the kingship of Mashiach, and therefore then is his hillula [day of passing and celebration]. And this itself is the aspect of the revelation of Mashiach, for &#8220;the sun rises and the sun sets&#8221; (Koheles 1:5) &mdash; &#8220;before the sun of one had set,&#8221; etc. (B&#8217;raishis Rabbah 58:2). And therefore, on Shavuos, when David was taken away, then immediately Mashiach was born and revealed &mdash; who will come speedily in our days, Amen.

&bull; &bull; &bull;

Translator&#8217;s Summary

This halacha is built on Likutay Moharan I:13 (&ldquo;Happy Is the People &mdash; Providence&rdquo;), one of Rabainu&rsquo;s most intricate teachings, which establishes that complete Divine Providence (Hashgachah) comes through receiving Torah, which comes through gathering souls, which comes through breaking the craving for money via tz&rsquo;dakah.

Reb Nosson maps the entire Pesach&ndash;S&rsquo;firah&ndash;Shavuos arc onto this chain: Pesach = Avraham / chesed / tz&rsquo;dakah / matzah = breaking the craving for money. The omer of barley = &ldquo;I have gathered my myrrh with my spice&rdquo; = gathering both the bitter souls (fallen desires = animal food) and the good souls. The S&rsquo;firah = bringing scattered souls under the count so that not one is lost. Shavuos = Atzeres (Assembly) = all souls finally gathered = Torah received = complete Hashgachah.

The Shavuos customs are decoded: staying awake all night = the &ldquo;Eye that does not slumber&rdquo; = breaking the &ldquo;sleep&rdquo; of the craving for money. Eating dairy = &ldquo;His eyes bathing in milk&rdquo; = the eyes of Providence receiving from the open Eye. Reading Rus = revelation of Mashiach / David&rsquo;s kingship. David&rsquo;s passing on Shavuos = the sun setting as the new sun of Mashiach rises.

The stunning wordplay: &ldquo;when he places his eye in the cup (bakos)&rdquo; / &ldquo;in the purse (bakis)&rdquo; = the craving for money blemishes wine (= the bloods) which blemishes the eyes (= Providence). And: &ldquo;silver refined, purified seven times&rdquo; = the 7&times;7 weeks of S&rsquo;firah purifying the &ldquo;silver&rdquo; of the craving for money, extracting the sparks, and making from them Torah.

Pesach: Avraham / Chesed / Matzah = Tz&rsquo;dakah &rarr; break the craving for money

&darr;

Omer of barley = &ldquo;I gathered my myrrh with my spice&rdquo; = gather bitter + good souls

&darr;

S&rsquo;firah = bring scattered souls under the count (none shall be lost)

&darr;

&ldquo;Silver refined, purified 7&times;7&rdquo; = sparks extracted from within the money

&darr;

Shavuos night: awake = &ldquo;Eye that does not slumber&rdquo; = complete Hashgachah

&darr;

Morning: dairy foods = &ldquo;His eyes bathing in milk&rdquo; / Rus = kingship of Mashiach

&darr;

Torah received = &ldquo;You have been shown&rdquo; = imaged in His Eyes

&darr;

Complete Divine Providence: &ldquo;Always the eyes of Hashem are upon it&rdquo;

&#10042; Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman &#10042;
